                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



  “Madame,” he began again.
  She played a note.



On the platform, in the ring, a man sat by himself in a gray jacket.
   I hadn’t stopped watching him. I don’t think I’d stopped for a
second. He sat with his hands in his lap, one shoulder high, and the
humming went up out of him in a thin, bluish thread. He wasn’t look‐
ing at the procession. He wasn’t looking at me. His eyes were on the
floor, as they’d been all night, exactly as they’d been on a stoop and in a
diner and on the fourth-floor landing.
   In the front row, a small woman in a church dress had left her seat.
   She was walking toward the platform.
   Nobody stopped her. The ushers had their hands out, and then, one
by one, lowered them. She crossed ten feet of white carpet in her flat
black shoes with her purse held in both hands, and she stopped at the
foot of the low round platform, below the ring, and looked up at her
son.
   She didn’t say anything.
   Mikey’s humming faltered.
   His head came up.
   The hall went quiet around him, the held-breath kind, where every‐
body knows something is about to be said. Manny, doubled over beside
me with his hand on his ribs, stopped drumming. The old woman’s
horn dropped to her chest. Even the attendants looked.
   “Mikey,” said Vale, very softly. The word came in over the micro‐
phone and over the speakers and under the horns, a low, patient warm
note, with a pause after it that opened like a door.
   Mikey’s head went down.
   His hands were shaking. The hum began again, stuttering, uncer‐
tain, his mouth closed on it like a hand on a rope.
   I looked at him across forty feet of carpet and thought of a hundred
things I could do. I could go to him. I could shout. I could put my hand
on the stone and ask it to take his, and I had a feeling it would. Every




